    {
      "id": "QkuZ0kVjvxGTOs_7uVTY9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 17850.40483221444,
      "y": 2810.771264235829,
      "width": 986.0392456054688,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bSn",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 439784014,
      "version": 1055,
      "versionNonce": 2003484498,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783100428812,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- load balancer in front of the API gateway \n    - load balancer: security club\n        - low level routine, SSL termination, basic network DDoS\n    - API gateway: the club bar attendant \n        - application-level concerns: JWT/Token validation, microservice routing, advanced rate limiting \n\n- horizontal scaling \n- caching layer for reads \n    - read-to-write ratio is massive (100:1/1000:1)\n    - introducing a high-performance, in-memory caching layer (Redis)\n    - user requests a short URL, check the cache firts\n        - only fetch from the db on cache miss, then write it back to the cache  \n\n- caching strategy \n    - eviction policy: LRU\n        - popular links stay hot, while old ones get cleared out \n    - cache-aside: you don't bloat the cache, you only add the ones people actually click\n            - create on cache-miss",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- load balancer in front of the API gateway \n    - load balancer: security club\n        - low level routine, SSL termination, basic network DDoS\n    - API gateway: the club bar attendant \n        - application-level concerns: JWT/Token validation, microservice routing, advanced rate limiting \n\n- horizontal scaling \n- caching layer for reads \n    - read-to-write ratio is massive (100:1/1000:1)\n    - introducing a high-performance, in-memory caching layer (Redis)\n    - user requests a short URL, check the cache firts\n        - only fetch from the db on cache miss, then write it back to the cache  \n\n- caching strategy \n    - eviction policy: LRU\n        - popular links stay hot, while old ones get cleared out \n    - cache-aside: you don't bloat the cache, you only add the ones people actually click\n            - create on cache-miss",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "AvS2oohcKUQL2I0JXjfBL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 19020.079652082615,
      "y": 2789.0956211054927,
      "width": 1564.5589599609375,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bSo",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1575989006,
      "version": 1263,
      "versionNonce": 571607118,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783100554873,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- on ID uniqueness discuss about a distributed ID generator \n    - e.g.: Key Gneration Service (KGS)\n    - the algo isn't enough. It runs into distributed concurrency flaw\n        - both keys generated by different servers might be equal \n            - this can either cause an error or one server can overwrite the other\n\n- separation of read vs. write paths\n    - write [creating a short link]: requites token generation, authentication checks, and db inserts\n    - read [redirect urls]: requires ultra fast lookup and 301/302 Redirect response \n    - dedicated servers or routes should handle them separately \n         - the read path shouldn't be blocked because the write one is experiencing high token generation latency  \n\n- for a URL shortener a NoSQL key-value store like DynamoDB or Cassandra is usually better than a relational db due to its massive horizontal read scalability\n  \n- HTTP status code:\n    - the servers should return 301 move permanently or 302 not found \n        - 301 if you want to let browsers cache the redirect and save your server traffic\n        - 302 if you want to track analytics on every single click ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- on ID uniqueness discuss about a distributed ID generator \n    - e.g.: Key Gneration Service (KGS)\n    - the algo isn't enough. It runs into distributed concurrency flaw\n        - both keys generated by different servers might be equal \n            - this can either cause an error or one server can overwrite the other\n\n- separation of read vs. write paths\n    - write [creating a short link]: requites token generation, authentication checks, and db inserts\n    - read [redirect urls]: requires ultra fast lookup and 301/302 Redirect response \n    - dedicated servers or routes should handle them separately \n         - the read path shouldn't be blocked because the write one is experiencing high token generation latency  \n\n- for a URL shortener a NoSQL key-value store like DynamoDB or Cassandra is usually better than a relational db due to its massive horizontal read scalability\n  \n- HTTP status code:\n    - the servers should return 301 move permanently or 302 not found \n        - 301 if you want to let browsers cache the redirect and save your server traffic\n        - 302 if you want to track analytics on every single click ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "aEF4JXnKqIprfQKotktO9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 20195.548886136046,
      "y": 2575.168874301711,
      "width": 1631.7989501953125,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bSr",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 384486606,
      "version": 771,
      "versionNonce": 1492693646,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783100820147,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to solve this in distributed systems?\n    - the server should know that its token is 100% globally true without checking the db \n\nAPPROACH A: key generation service \n- instead of generating tokens on the fly, you create a separate, lightweight microservice (KGS) whose only job is to maintain a sequence of pre-generated unique keys\n    - KGS keeps a chunk of keys in its local memory \n    - when url service needs to create a key [short url], it just asks the kgs: \"give me an unused token\"\n        - kgs hands it over instantly \n        - no db lookups/no loops/zero chance of duplication\n\nAPPROACH B: distributed counter + base62 encoding \n      ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to solve this in distributed systems?\n    - the server should know that its token is 100% globally true without checking the db \n\nAPPROACH A: key generation service \n- instead of generating tokens on the fly, you create a separate, lightweight microservice (KGS) whose only job is to maintain a sequence of pre-generated unique keys\n    - KGS keeps a chunk of keys in its local memory \n    - when url service needs to create a key [short url], it just asks the kgs: \"give me an unused token\"\n        - kgs hands it over instantly \n        - no db lookups/no loops/zero chance of duplication\n\nAPPROACH B: distributed counter + base62 encoding \n      ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
  "appState": {
